-- DX EXPECTATION (not necessarily spec-required): a data declaration
-- truncated right after its trailing `=` (file ends before any
-- constructor) should say the constructor block / expression after `=`
-- is missing.  §3.1.14A lists "missing expression after `=`" and
-- "missing indentation block after a block introducer" as recoverable
-- conditions, and §8.4's block form introduces constructors with
-- exactly this trailing `=`.
--
-- WHAT CURRENTLY HAPPENS (a0497523): the whole declaration is skipped
-- with E_RECOVERY_DECLARATION_SKIPPED "unexpected 'data' at start of
-- declaration", anchored at the `data` keyword itself.  `data` at the
-- start of a declaration is perfectly legal — the message blames the
-- one token the user got right and never mentions the missing
-- constructor list, inviting users to "fix" the head of the
-- declaration instead of its truncated tail.
--! mode check
--! assertErrorCount 1
--! assertDiagnosticMatch constructor|expression|after '='
module main

data Foo : Type =
-- STATUS(a0497523): FAIL — E_RECOVERY_DECLARATION_SKIPPED "unexpected 'data' at start of declaration"; nothing mentions the missing constructor block
